        "original_id": "f8bf190a-fc36-5bc2-9eb1-b8b942668c72",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Joseph A. Citro\n- Places: Bennington Triangle, Bridgewater Triangle, southwestern Vermont, southeastern Massachusetts\n- Topics: missing persons, alleged paranormal phenomena\n\nFacts:\n- Between 1920 and 1950, a number of people went missing in an area of southwestern Vermont later called the Bennington Triangle.\n- In 1992, New England author Joseph A. Citro coined the phrase \"Bennington Triangle\" during a public radio broadcast.\n- Joseph A. Citro said the Bennington Triangle shares characteristics with the Bridgewater Triangle in neighboring Massachusetts.\n- The Bridgewater Triangle is an area of about 200 square miles in southeastern Massachusetts in the United States.\n- The Bridgewater Triangle is claimed to be a site of alleged paranormal phenomena, including UFOs, poltergeists, orbs, balls of fire, other spectral phenomena, bigfoot-like sightings, giant snakes, and thunderbirds.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4e5adee6-80c7-52bc-824e-5c8408b2f088",
        "text": "Page: Bennington Triangle. Sentence: \"Bennington Triangle\" is a phrase coined by New England author Joseph A. Citro during a public radio broadcast in 1992 to denote an area of southwestern Vermont within which a number of people went missing between 1920 and 1950.\nPage: Bennington Triangle. Sentence: According to Citro, the area shares characteristics with the Bridgewater Triangle in neighboring Massachusetts.\nPage: Bridgewater Triangle. Sentence: The Bridgewater Triangle refers to an area of about 200 mi2 within southeastern Massachusetts in the United States, claimed to be a site of alleged paranormal phenomena, ranging from UFOs to poltergeists, orbs, balls of fire and other spectral phenomena, various bigfoot-like sightings, giant snakes and \"thunderbirds.\"",
